// ML-025a: real integer-format scanf() E2E, mirroring musl_printf_int.test's
// pipeline (real clang --target=dadao compile, real ld.lld link against
// crt1.o + libc.a, real QEMU/gem5 dual-backend run). The RUN lines below
// assert the INTENDED (bug-fixed) behavior of scanf("%d", &x): read "42"
// from stdin (fed via ordinary shell stdin redirection -- there is no
// existing lit.cfg stdin substitution to reuse, and none was needed: plain
// `<` redirection composes fine inside the existing `bash -c '...'` RUN
// pattern every other E2E test already uses for stdout redirection),
// parse it, print it back, and exit with that value.
//
// Expected to fail (see the XFAIL directive below): this currently fails
// on BOTH backends (QEMU and gem5 agree:
// process exits 129 == 0x81 == MALIGN, or some other non-42 exit and/or
// wrong stdout content, depending on exact code shape -- see below) --
// NOT because of this task's own work (the 6 softfloat symbols
// __extendsfdf2/__truncdfsf2/__floatsisf/__mulsf3/__divdf3/__gedf2 this
// task added to close the link gap are independently verified correct:
// ~2.1M host-fuzz cases against native hardware float/double arithmetic,
// 0 mismatches, plus disassembly confirms 0 self-recursion and 0 new
// undefined-symbol dependencies -- see docs/issues-archive.yaml's closed
// `musl-vfscanf-missing-single-precision-and-divide-softfloat-symbols`
// entry). Root cause is the separate, pre-existing, already-tracked
// `varargs-pointer-args-lost-rb-bank-save-area` bug (docs/issues.yaml):
// `&x` is an output-type pointer passed as scanf's variadic argument, and
// LLVM's varargs save-area prologue only spills the RD register bank,
// never the RB bank a pointer argument actually lives in, so va_arg reads
// back garbage instead of the real address. This is unavoidable for ANY
// scanf()/vscanf() call with a conversion specifier (unlike printf's %d,
// which passes an int by value and has no pointer-vararg involved at all
// -- see musl_printf_int.test, which is why THAT test could dodge this gap
// by sticking to integer format specifiers, but scanf has no equivalent
// "pass by value" form to dodge to). Confirmed via an isolated negative
// control: sscanf("hello", "hello") (zero pointer varargs, no conversion
// specifiers) runs clean on both backends, proving vfscanf/intscan parsing
// itself is sound and the fault is 100% attributable to the pointer
// vararg.
//
// This test is intentionally kept (not deleted/skipped) so it flips to an
// unexpected pass -- and demands attention -- the day the
// varargs-pointer-args-lost-rb-bank-save-area fix lands.
// XFAIL: *
//
// RUN: %clang --target=dadao -std=c99 -nostdinc -ffreestanding -O2 \
// RUN:   -I%S/../../../.work/source/musl/arch/dadao \
// RUN:   -I%S/../../../.work/source/musl/arch/generic \
// RUN:   -I%S/../../../.work/source/musl/include \
// RUN:   -I%S/../../../.work/build/musl/obj/include \
// RUN:   -c -o %t.o %S/Inputs/musl_scanf_int.c
// RUN: %ld.lld -T %S/../../scripts/dadao.ld \
// RUN:   --start-group %S/../../../.work/build/musl/lib/crt1.o %t.o %S/../../../.work/build/musl/lib/libc.a --end-group \
// RUN:   -o %t.elf
// RUN: %llvm-objcopy -O binary %t.elf %t.bin
// RUN: echo 42 > %t.stdin
// RUN: bash -c '%qemu -M dadao-m1 -display none -nodefaults -bios %trampoline -kernel %t.bin < %t.stdin > %t.out 2>&1; test $? -eq 42'
// RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MARKER < %t.out
// RUN: bash -c '%gem5 %gem5_se %t.elf < %t.stdin > %t.gout 2>&1; test $? -eq 42'
// RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MARKER < %t.gout
//
// MARKER: got=42
